WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.153
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:08.839
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.139 --> 00:00:16.679
Unit seventeen: Quantitative Research Methods. Today you will report quantitative procedures with passive constructions.

4
00:00:16.979 --> 00:00:19.562
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.582
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:25.812
Hypothesis.

7
00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:31.185
Hypothesis means a testable prediction about the relationship between variables.

8
00:00:31.783 --> 00:00:34.212
The hypothesis was partially supported.

9
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:37.492
Your turn. Say: Hypothesis.

10
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:41.636
Hypothesis.

11
00:00:42.383 --> 00:00:43.315
Variable.

12
00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:47.756
Variable means a characteristic that can be measured numerically.

13
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.000
The dependent variable was reading speed.

14
00:00:51.583 --> 00:00:53.992
Your turn. Say: Variable.

15
00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:57.811
Variable.

16
00:00:58.550 --> 00:00:59.609
Survey.

17
00:01:00.617 --> 00:01:04.323
Survey means a standardised questionnaire administered to a sample.

18
00:01:04.917 --> 00:01:06.881
The survey was administered online.

19
00:01:07.483 --> 00:01:09.973
Your turn. Say: Survey.

20
00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:13.788
Survey.

21
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:15.621
Data set.

22
00:01:16.617 --> 00:01:19.615
Data set means a structured collection of related data.

23
00:01:20.217 --> 00:01:22.959
The national data set covers twenty years.

24
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:26.191
Your turn. Say: Data set.

25
00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:30.069
Data set.

26
00:01:30.817 --> 00:01:31.818
Regression.

27
00:01:32.817 --> 00:01:37.591
Regression means a statistical method for modelling relationships between variables.

28
00:01:38.183 --> 00:01:40.357
Regression controlled for age and income.

29
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.440
Your turn. Say: Regression.

30
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.440
Regression.

31
00:01:48.183 --> 00:01:49.428
Reliability.

32
00:01:50.417 --> 00:01:54.610
Reliability means the consistency of a measure across repeated tests.

33
00:01:55.217 --> 00:01:57.425
Test-retest reliability was high.

34
00:01:58.017 --> 00:02:01.191
Your turn. Say: Reliability.

35
00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:05.325
Reliability.

36
00:02:06.083 --> 00:02:07.073
Validity.

37
00:02:08.083 --> 00:02:12.962
Validity means the accuracy with which a tool measures its intended concept.

38
00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.200
Validity was threatened by ambiguous questions.

39
00:02:16.783 --> 00:02:19.053
Your turn. Say: Validity.

40
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.006
Validity.

41
00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:25.285
Generalizability.

42
00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:30.175
Generalizability means the extent to which findings apply beyond the sample.

43
00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:34.025
Generalizability is limited to urban schools.

44
00:02:34.617 --> 00:02:37.733
Your turn. Say: Generalizability.

45
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.112
Generalizability.

46
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.377
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.217 --> 00:02:51.051
Visal, could you summarise the methods section of your quantitative study?

48
00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:59.690
Certainly. The hypothesis was tested using a large data set from the national education survey.

49
00:03:00.783 --> 00:03:02.934
How were other factors accounted for?

50
00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:10.961
A regression model was applied, so that age, income and school type were controlled for.

51
00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:14.468
And how confident are you in the results?

52
00:03:15.583 --> 00:03:25.910
Reasonably. Reliability was confirmed by a retest, although generalizability is limited because only urban schools were included.

53
00:03:27.067 --> 00:03:28.799
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:33.635
Grammar focus: Passive constructions for methodology.

55
00:03:33.935 --> 00:03:35.203
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:35.917 --> 00:03:39.054
The hypothesis was tested using a large data set.

57
00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.762
A regression model was applied to control for income.

58
00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.184
Reliability was confirmed by a retest.

59
00:03:47.133 --> 00:03:56.334
Use the past passive to report what was done. Phrasal-prepositional verbs keep their particles in the passive: 'income was controlled for', 'outliers were accounted for'.

60
00:03:57.083 --> 00:04:01.930
Be careful! Don't say: The hypothesis was tested use a data set.

61
00:04:03.867 --> 00:04:07.852
Say: The hypothesis was tested using a data set.

62
00:04:08.152 --> 00:04:09.954
Use 'using' to name the method.

63
00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:13.384
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:15.515
Counting What Counts.

65
00:04:15.815 --> 00:04:20.960
Quantitative research aims to measure relationships precisely and test them objectively.

66
00:04:21.967 --> 00:04:28.889
In a typical study, the hypothesis was tested using a large data set and a pre-registered analysis plan.

67
00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:34.198
Regression models were applied so that confounding variables could be controlled for.

68
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:41.193
Reliability concerns whether results are consistent, whereas validity concerns whether they are accurate.

69
00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:47.752
Even a reliable and valid study, however, may have limited generalizability beyond its sample.

70
00:04:48.867 --> 00:04:53.240
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:54.083 --> 00:04:57.221
The hypothesis was blank using a large data set.

72
00:04:57.521 --> 00:05:01.058
A, tested. B, testing. C, test.

73
00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:06.692
The answer is A: tested.

74
00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:10.059
Age and income blank controlled for.

75
00:05:10.359 --> 00:05:13.513
A, was. B, were. C, been.

76
00:05:17.067 --> 00:05:18.799
The answer is B: were.

77
00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:25.064
A scale gives the same weight every time but it is wrong. It is reliable but not blank.

78
00:05:25.364 --> 00:05:29.133
A, consistent. B, valid. C, large.

79
00:05:32.667 --> 00:05:34.724
The answer is B: valid.

80
00:05:35.883 --> 00:05:38.812
Which passive sentence keeps the particle correctly?

81
00:05:39.112 --> 00:05:45.493
A, Outliers were accounted.. B, Outliers were accounted for.. C, Outliers were for accounted..

82
00:05:49.033 --> 00:05:52.240
The answer is B: Outliers were accounted for..

83
00:05:53.383 --> 00:05:57.508
According to the reading, what may limit even a reliable and valid study?

84
00:05:57.808 --> 00:06:02.587
A, generalizability. B, regression. C, pre-registration.

85
00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:08.783
The answer is A: generalizability.

86
00:06:09.967 --> 00:06:13.408
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:17.887
What is the difference between reliability and validity in research?

88
00:06:34.683 --> 00:06:36.195
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:36.495 --> 00:07:11.780
Reliability refers to consistency: if a test is repeated under the same conditions, the same results should be obtained. Validity, by contrast, refers to accuracy, meaning whether the test actually measures what it was designed to measure. For example, a bathroom scale that always shows two kilos too much is reliable but not valid. Some people treat the two terms as synonyms, which causes confusion. In fact, good research requires both.

90
00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.986
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:14.883 --> 00:07:20.043
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive constructions for methodology.

92
00:07:20.343 --> 00:07:24.261
Today's value is precision. Choose your words and numbers with care.

93
00:07:24.561 --> 00:07:27.330
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.491
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

